<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Stargard</h1><p class="st-meta">1352 CE – 1471 CE · capital Neubrandenburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A junior Mecklenburg duchy around Neubrandenburg and Stargard that lasted just over a century before reuniting with the main line.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1352 the brothers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-ii-duke-of-mecklenburg">Albert II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-duke-of-mecklenburg-stargard">John I</a>, raised to dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg</a> in 1348, divided their lands. John received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lordship-of-stargard">Lordship of Stargard</a> in the east, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neubrandenburg">Neubrandenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a>, which Mecklenburg had acquired from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in the 13th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The duchy was an agrarian land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knighthood">knightly</a> estates and small towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neubrandenburg">Neubrandenburg</a>, with its brick Gothic gates, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedland-mecklenburg">Friedland</a> and Woldegk. Its dukes remained junior partners of their cousins in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg">Duchy of Mecklenburg</a> and joined in their wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Pomerania</a>. Few details of its administration survive.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-ii-duke-of-mecklenburg-stargard">Ulrich II</a> died without sons in 1471, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lordship-of-stargard">Stargard</a> reverted to the main <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg</a> line. The region re-emerged in 1701 as the core of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-strelitz">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Strelitz</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="duchy, 1352 CE – 1471 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">duchy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:86.61%;width:2.37%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1352 CE – 1471 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Neubrandenburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John I, Ulrich II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mecklenburg-Stargard</dd></dl></aside></div>
